  "limitations": [
    "Heat lamps and fans were used for simulating convective and radiative heat transfer; conductive thermal apparatus should also be considered in future research.",
    "Humidity can be added in MIVE simulation with HVAC systems in the climate chamber or local humidifier device.",
    "A benchmark between the actual environment and the virtual environment was not provided; future validation studies comparing multi-sensory perceptions, perceived presence, and behaviours between actual and MIVE exposure are needed.",
    "Calibration of auditory playback in relation to the actual recording can be done using a head and torso simulator wearing the VR headset in future studies.",
    "The sense of smell, important for holistic experience, deserves further exploration and integration into Virtual Reality.",
    "Users' perceived realism for the three visual environmental layers (immediate, contextual, and atmospheric environment) can also be assessed and benchmarked in future studies."
  ],
  "minimum_safe_summary": "This paper develops a novel multisensory immersive virtual reality (MIVE) methodology to study human-built environment interactions under multimodal (visual, auditory, and thermal) conditions, aiming to bridge the gap between experimental control and ecological validity. Key findings from a case study application demonstrate that MIVE can induce significant differences in thermal pleasure and physiological arousal between sunlight-only and sunlight-plus-shade scenarios, with participants showing behavioral preferences for shaded areas. The MIVE environment also achieved a high sense of presence. Limitations include the need for future research to incorporate conductive thermal apparatus, humidity, olfactory sense, and to conduct validation studies comparing MIVE experiences with actual environments.",
  "crossref_title": "An immersive multisensory virtual reality approach to the study of human-built environment interactions",
